          class="font-medium underline hover:text-amber-700">our bulletin</a
        > for what we know so far.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Already have a GM-15 Pro or UV-5G?</strong> Nothing changes for you. Your radio still works
      on our repeaters and nets, and there is no reason to replace it.
    </p>

    <h3>If You Need a Radio Now</h3>

    <p>
      If you can wait until December, we suggest waiting. We have not yet tested or programmed the
      radios below ourselves, so setup may take more effort than with the radios we have been using,
      and this list may change. If you need a radio before then, these are GMRS-specific,
      repeater-capable handhelds with programmable channels, sold by their makers or by established
      US dealers. Prices are as of late November 2026.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.buytwowayradios.com/wouxun-kg-805g.html" target="_blank"
          >Wouxun KG-805G</a
        > (about $80 from Buy Two Way Radios): a simple, sturdy radio with 128 programmable channels.
        In stock when we checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.buytwowayradios.com/wouxun-kg-935g-plus.html" target="_blank"
          >Wouxun KG-935G Plus</a
        > (about $120 from Buy Two Way Radios): a step up, with a color display, 999 programmable channels,
        and better weather resistance. Backordered into early December when we checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://baofengtech.com/product/gmrs-v2/" target="_blank">BTECH GMRS-V2</a> (about $65
        direct from BTECH): the closest relative of the radios we have been using, with 200 programmable
        channels and Chirp support. Backordered into mid-December when we checked.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>If you are on a tighter budget, these lower-cost radios are also worth a look:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.radioddity.com/products/radioddity-gm-30-plus" target="_blank"
          >Radioddity GM-30 Plus</a
        > (about $37 direct from Radioddity): the least expensive option here, with 1,000 programmable
        channels and Chirp support.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://powerwerx.com/anytone-handheld-radios" target="_blank">AnyTone UVA1 GMRS</a
        >
        (about $50 from Powerwerx): a compact radio with a color display. Make sure you order the GMRS
        version. A nearly identical ham radio is sold under the same UVA1 name and will not work for GMRS.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.retevis.com/products/ra89g-high-power-gmrs-handheld-ham-radio-with-bluetooth-app-programming"
          target="_blank">Retevis RA89G</a
        > (about $60 to $70 direct from Retevis): a newer radio with a large display, 200 programmable
        channels, and programming from a phone app. Reviewers report that it is not weather sealed, so
        keep it dry.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      GMRS radios sold in stores by brands like Midland are another option, but many support only a
      limited set of tones and custom channels. Please email <a href="mailto:ersnnets@gmail.com"
        >ersnnets@gmail.com</a
      > before buying one so we can help you check that it will work with our repeaters.
    </p>

    <p>Whatever you buy, a few checks will keep you out of trouble:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Make sure it is a GMRS radio.</strong> The listing should say GMRS and "repeater capable."
        Many similar-looking radios, including the popular Baofeng UV-5R, are ham radios that are not
        certified for GMRS.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Look for the FCC ID.</strong> A certified radio has an FCC ID printed on its label and
        usually in its listing. You can look it up with the <a
          href="https://www.fcc.gov/oet/ea/fccid"
          target="_blank">FCC ID search</a
        > and confirm it is approved for GMRS (Part 95E).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Don't overpay.</strong> Be wary of marked-up resale listings for the radios that have
        disappeared. Good alternatives cost about the same.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What to Look For</h3>

    <p>
      If you're interested in diving deeper into radio programming and exploring advanced options,
      here’s some additional information to help guide your decision-making. Choosing the right GMRS
      radio will depend on your intended use, environment, and budget. Here are key factors to
      consider when purchasing a GMRS radio:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Range and Power:</strong> Look for radios with higher wattage if you need extended range,
        especially in open or mountainous areas. GMRS radios can legally go up to 50 watts, but most handheld
        units are in the 1-5 watt range, which typically covers a few miles under normal conditions. Look
        for radios with repeater capability if you plan to join the ERSN net.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Durability and Weather Resistance:</strong> For outdoor or rugged use, prioritize radios
        with waterproof or dustproof ratings (IPX5 or higher) to ensure longevity in harsh conditions.
        Models designed for outdoor enthusiasts often come with durable casings to withstand impacts and
        rough handling.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Battery Life:</strong> Check for long-lasting battery life or dual-power options that
        allow both rechargeable and disposable batteries. Radios with USB charging ports can add convenience
        for recharging on the go.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Ease of Use and Display:</strong> Radios with clear displays and intuitive controls make
        it easier to tune channels and adjust settings.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Additional Features:</strong> Some radios include NOAA weather alerts and SOS functionality.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Cost:</strong> GMRS radios vary widely in price. Entry-level handheld models start around
        $30, while high-powered mobile units or models with extensive features can exceed $200. Choose
        a model that balances your needs and budget.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Popular brands include Motorola, Midland, and BTech, each offering models that cater to
      different needs from casual family use to advanced outdoor applications. Be sure to research
      specific models and read reviews to find a radio that fits your requirements.
    </p>
  </Content>
